What are the features?

The clues we feed the model to help it decide:

Transaction amount
Location of the purchase
Time of day / day of week
Merchant category (gas station, online store, etc.)
Device or IP address used
How different this transaction is from the customer's usual spending habits

What is the label?

Is this transaction fraud? Yes or No

The model should be able to predict user to user, whether or not the account signing in, is the owner or a fraud

What would training data contain?

Our training data would consist of a large number of previous transactions, already labelled as either fraud or not fraud. For each transaction we would have all of the attributes described previously (time, amount, device, location etc.) as well as the correct answer (fraud/not fraud). This is essentially the answer key that the system will learn from during training before being put onto new transactions it has not seen before.

Why should testing data be kept separate?

We also need to use separate data to test our system to try to determine whether the system has actually learned the relationship or has simply overlearned the training data. For instance, a student might take a practice test in an attempt to learn a subject. If that student then takes the same test they would certainly do well, but this would not indicate that the student has learned the material. Using transactions that the system has not seen before during testing will give a more accurate representation of the system's performance on future, completely new, fraud transactions.

What could be a false positive?

A false positive occurs when an otherwise legitimate transaction is classified as fraudulent by the system. For instance, a consumer may purchase a high end item while travelling but the system may block the transaction because the transaction is believed to be thief involved, when in fact it is merely the consumer. This can cause dissatisfaction in the consumer and a sense of mistrust towards the organisation.

What could be a false negative?

A false negative occurs when actual fraud is not detected and is allowed to pass through as non-fraud. In this case a fraudulent user will use a credit card for a small purchase that appears harmless and the system will fail to detect the transaction as fraudulent resulting in a loss for the organisation.

Would precision or recall be more important?

Recall would be more important

In fraud we typically want to avoid a false negative (missing genuine fraud) more than a false positive (flagging a good transaction). A false negative costs the company money and hurts the customer. A false positive is annoying but relatively easy to deal with (you can send a "Was this you?" text message). Hence we want to cast a slightly broader net and catch more genuine fraud (which will include some false positives). We therefore care more about recall (amount of genuine fraud caught) than **precision** (all flagged transactions are certainly fraud).

What types of poor-quality data could affect this model?

Missing information — some transactions missing location or device data
Mislabeled data — a fraud case accidentally marked as "normal" in the training data, or vice versa
Inconsistent formats — different systems recording currency, timestamps, or merchant names differently
Outdated data — old fraud patterns that don't reflect how scammers operate today
Imbalanced data — way more normal transactions than fraud ones, making fraud hard for the model to learn

What could cause this model to overfit?

Overfitting occurs when a model "memorises" the training data rather than learning the underlying rules. For example a student may memorise the correct answers to a test rather than learning the underlying knowledge. This may occur because:

The training data is too small or doesn't have enough variety

The model is too complex and starts learning random noise or coincidences (like "fraud always happens at 3:47 PM" just because that's what happened in training)

The same customers or transactions appear multiple times in training

There isn't enough real fraud data, so the model latches onto tiny, unreliable patterns

What would make you trust—or not trust—the model's predictions?

I would have more faith in this model if:

- The model demonstrates good performance on new and unobserved transactions in addition to observed transactions used for training
- The model is frequently evaluated using new fraudulent transactions
- The features used in the model are reasonable and not based on any biased or arbitrary information
- Flagged transactions are reviewed by a human before taking any significant action (such as freezing a user's account)

I would have less faith in this model if:

- The model has not been updated to include new transactions
- Certain classes of users are flagged unfairly more frequently than other classes of users
- The model cannot explain a reason for flagging a particular transaction ("black box")
- There is a significant rate of false positives or false negatives on real transactions
